  "curation": {
    "architecture_summary": "simple_thinking_tool is a Python 3.12 MCP (Model Context Protocol) server that exposes a catalog of 15 structured thinking methods as individual MCP tools, plus 3 utility tools (health, definitions, journal). The architecture follows a hub-and-spoke pattern: server.py is the central entry point importing 6 internal modules (auth, config, guard, hooks, modes, store); service.py is the secondary business-logic hub importing auth, modes, and store. Modes are data-driven via modes.json (add a JSON entry, get a new think_<mode> tool with no code change). The server never calls an LLM, makes zero outbound network calls, and performs no reasoning — it fills string templates from caller-supplied fields and appends rendered notes to an append-only JSONL file with fsync. Optional cross-cutting features include a loop guard (prevents agent churn), fire-and-forget background hooks, and bearer-token auth for HTTP transport. The package ships as pip-installable with stdio (default) or HTTP transport, designed for single-instance private deployment per agent. Test coverage is 1:1 — each source module has a corresponding test file, with test_server.py and test_service.py additionally importing secondary modules (modes and store respectively).",
    "clusters": [
      {
        "name": "Core Server & Dispatch",
        "members": [
          "file:src/simple_thinking_tool/__init__.py",
          "file:src/simple_thinking_tool/server.py"
        ],
        "rationale": "Package initialization and MCP server entry point. server.py is the import hub that pulls in all other modules, registers tools, and dispatches requests. __init__.py provides package metadata."
      },
      {
        "name": "Business Logic & Modes",
        "members": [
          "file:src/simple_thinking_tool/service.py",
          "file:src/simple_thinking_tool/modes.py",
          "file:src/simple_thinking_tool/modes.json"
        ],
        "rationale": "The core thinking logic. service.py fills templates and orchestrates guard/hook/store calls. modes.py loads and validates mode definitions from modes.json and dynamically registers them as MCP tools. modes.json is the data-driven catalog of all 15 thinking modes."
      },
      {
        "name": "Persistence Layer",
        "members": [
          "file:src/simple_thinking_tool/store.py"
        ],
        "rationale": "Isolated JSONL append-only storage with fsync, log rotation, and journal read-back with filters. The sole persistence concern, imported by both server.py and service.py."
      },
      {
        "name": "Cross-cutting Security & Optional Features",
        "members": [
